<meta type=article title="Opt-in Enscribe markup in data-table cells (#21)">
</meta>

<section | A parsed prose column>

Data-format tables hold data, so cells are literal by default. A `parse-columns`
attribute opts a single prose column into Enscribe inline markup while the data
columns stay literal. Long form is used (the cell content carries `>`, which
would end pipe content early). The notes column links, emphasises, cross-refs the
figure below, and cites — all parsed; `sample` and `value` stay literal.

<fig #fig:plot src="plot.png" caption="A reference plot, cross-referenced from a table cell." />

<table #tab:results csv parse-columns="notes" caption="Results — only the notes column parses.">
sample,value,notes
A,3.14,see *the docs* at <a https://example.org | the site>
B,2.72,compare with <ref @fig:plot> and <cite @smith2020>
C,1.41,uses `inline code` and inline math $x^2$
</table>

<section | Parsing every cell>

With `+parse-text`, every cell parses — the all-cells opt-in.

<table csv +parse-text caption="Every cell parsed.">
label,detail
*emphasised label*,a link to <a https://example.org | the site>
</table>

<section | Explicit opt-out>

`-parse-text` keeps a table literal even if a document-wide default turned
parsing on. Here it is literal (no global default set), demonstrating the
attribute is accepted and leaves cells untouched.

<table csv -parse-text caption="Explicitly literal.">
token,note
x,this *stays* literal and <a https://example.org | this> is not a link
</table>

<data>
<library |
@article{smith2020, author = {Smith, Ada}, title = {On Cells}, journal = {J. Tables}, year = {2020}}
>
</data>

<bibliography>
</bibliography>
